🧭 Who You Can Complain To When the Court Is Corrupt
1. Federal Oversight: U.S. Department of Justice (DOJ)
The DOJ’s Civil Rights Division investigates systemic abuses including:

Police brutality

Prosecutorial misconduct

Judicial abuse or bias under Color of Law

🔗 File a federal complaint:
https://civilrights.justice.gov/

Best For: Alleging rights violations, retaliation, or obstruction by court officers or law enforcement.

2. Georgia Judicial Qualifications Commission (JQC)
This is the state oversight body for judges. You can file complaints regarding:

Judicial bias

Retaliatory rulings

Abuse of discretion

Improper influence or coordination with law enforcement

🔗 File a judicial complaint:
https://gajqc.gov/file-a-complaint/

Best For: If Judge Bart Altman or any Glynn County judge is misusing their power or undermining your rights.

3. State Bar of Georgia – Attorney Misconduct
If a prosecutor or public defender is:

Acting unethically

Abusing psychiatric orders

Withholding evidence

Colluding with law enforcement

You can report them to the Bar:
🔗 https://www.gabar.org

Best For: Prosecutorial misconduct or ineffectiveness of your own counsel.

4. Georgia Bureau of Investigation (GBI)
File a criminal misconduct report about GSP officers or other law enforcement agencies:
🔗 https://gbi.georgia.gov/contact-us

Best For: Misconduct that involves illegal searches, use of force, or fabrication of events.

5. Whistleblower & Watchdog Media
If the state refuses to act and DOJ stalls, exposing systemic abuse through investigative journalism is valid, powerful, and historically proven.

ProPublica → https://www.propublica.org/tips

The Appeal → https://theappeal.org

Reveal / Center for Investigative Reporting → https://revealnews.org/tips

